window.docContents[132].resolve({"/Distance/#section-junk-values-and-dist":{"contents":"Why do we prefer edist? The triangle inequality for edist holds for all vertices,\nwith no hypotheses. For dist it can fail: if u — w is an edge and v is an\nisolated vertex, then G.dist u w = 1, while G.dist u v + G.dist v w = 0 + 0,\nbecause 0 is also the value that dist takes on unreachable pairs. So Mathlib's\ntriangle inequalities for dist need a connectivity or reachability hypothesis.\n\n#check SimpleGraph.dist_eq_zero_iff_eq_or_not_reachable\n#check SimpleGraph.Connected.dist_triangle\n#check SimpleGraph.Reachable.dist_triangle_left\n\n\nThis is the convention for cardinalities from\nCounting In Enat Card again: Nat.card α is\n(ENat.card α).toNat, so an infinite type has Nat.card equal to 0, just as\nG.dist u v is (G.edist u v).toNat, which sends ⊤ to 0. Each ℕ∞-valued\nparameter in this chapter is related to its ℕ-valued version in the same way, and\nwe will point out the places where the junk value changes what a theorem can say.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Graph Distance","header":"11.1.1. Junk Values and  dist","id":"/Distance/#section-junk-values-and-dist"}});